1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,401
Here's how to reverse engineer Productboard from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,401 --> 00:00:08,450
Claude passed all twenty-five checks, but my spec never said to hide sign-in links from admins.

3
00:00:09,217 --> 00:00:10,945
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:11,137 --> 00:00:13,996
It copies the part of Productboard a small team actually needs.

5
00:00:14,337 --> 00:00:17,473
One inbox for customer feedback, in the customer's own words.

6
00:00:17,793 --> 00:00:20,202
Each quote tied to an account and its monthly revenue.

7
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:23,059
Themes ranked by evidence, revenue and votes.

8
00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:26,070
And when a theme ships, a list of every customer to tell.

9
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:29,326
The core rule is how revenue counts.

10
00:00:29,624 --> 00:00:36,042
Acme Corp pays two thousand four hundred dollars a month, and asked for single sign-on twice, in a ticket and on a call.

11
00:00:36,231 --> 00:00:40,476
That's two pieces of evidence, but the theme's revenue stays at the same amount, not double.

12
00:00:40,754 --> 00:00:42,631
Each account counts once per theme.

13
00:00:42,844 --> 00:00:44,672
And a vote never adds revenue.

14
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,374
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

15
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:51,616
The foundation, with customers, people and the outbox.

16
00:00:51,894 --> 00:00:54,304
Capture, the inbound endpoint and triage.

17
00:00:54,582 --> 00:00:57,376
Themes, votes, promotion and the roadmap.

18
00:00:57,611 --> 00:00:59,807
Then closing the loop, and CSV export.

19
00:00:59,997 --> 00:01:04,521
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

20
00:01:05,617 --> 00:01:06,513
Here's what it built.

21
00:01:06,812 --> 00:01:12,230
The themes screen ranks single sign-on first, with two pieces of evidence, one account and two votes.

22
00:01:12,529 --> 00:01:14,682
The theme page keeps its full status history.

23
00:01:14,915 --> 00:01:18,286
The inbox keeps each quote with its customer, revenue and source.

24
00:01:18,627 --> 00:01:21,507
The roadmap has five lanes, with an owner on every card.

25
00:01:21,742 --> 00:01:24,935
And when a theme ships, follow-ups lists every account to tell.

26
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:33,138
In one final run, on a fresh database, all twenty-five checks passed, along with one hundred and fifty-seven automated tests.

27
00:01:34,217 --> 00:01:36,201
But the more useful result is what broke.

28
00:01:36,479 --> 00:01:38,548
The build exposed twenty gaps in the spec.

29
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:40,297
Places where Claude had to guess.

30
00:01:40,617 --> 00:01:44,244
The outbox was in no phase prompt, yet later checks read emails there.

31
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:46,264
The seed people had no email addresses.

32
00:01:46,449 --> 00:01:49,094
And the CSV exports had no exact columns.

33
00:01:49,308 --> 00:01:51,093
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

34
00:01:52,183 --> 00:01:54,444
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

35
00:01:54,657 --> 00:02:00,716
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the hourly retry job on a real scheduler, or hosting.

36
00:02:00,929 --> 00:02:03,024
Those are yours to check before your team relies on it.

37
00:02:04,117 --> 00:02:05,930
So is it worth building?

38
00:02:06,506 --> 00:02:08,149
Productboard doesn't publish prices.

39
00:02:08,383 --> 00:02:13,544
Its Business plan is sold through a demo, with a minimum of five makers, and Enterprise goes through sales.

40
00:02:13,755 --> 00:02:20,241
If you need native integrations with tools like Zendesk, Gong or Salesforce, or a portal where customers vote, buy Productboard.

41
00:02:20,497 --> 00:02:23,938
If you just need to know who asked, and who to tell when it ships, build it.

42
00:02:25,017 --> 00:02:26,361
Everything is in one file.

43
00:02:26,596 --> 00:02:33,598
The data model, fifteen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty-five checks, with the twenty fixes already in.

44
00:02:34,467 --> 00:02:38,007
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

45
00:02:38,007 --> 00:02:40,167
If you build it, tell me what broke.

46
00:02:40,167 --> 00:02:40,647
I'm Sam.

47
00:02:40,647 --> 00:02:41,667
See you tomorrow.
